  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how mechanical force can activate embedded chemical groups in polymer networks and convert that force into new reactions inside soft materials. Overall, it compares several gel designs in which stretching or stress breaks or opens specific “mechanophore” units along polymer chains, generating reactive species that then change the network structure.\n\nIn the top scheme, a dibromocyclopropane unit appears to undergo force-triggered ring opening. In a polymer network, this activation is accompanied by some chain scission, creating reactive chain sites; a subsequent crosslinking step reconnects chains into a modified network. The cartoons emphasize a transition from a closed, intact motif to an open, reactive one and then to new links between chains.\n\nThe lower left panel shows a double-network gel, where force seems to generate radicals from weak bonds in one network. An inset suggests cleavage of a central bond to produce radical-ended chains.\n\nThe lower right panel depicts cyanofluorene-based radicals formed under force. These radicals then initiate polymerization of side chains, increasing connectivity through new chain growth in addition to existing physical crosslinks. Across the figure, the main trend is that mechanical stress is used not just to damage polymers, but to trigger useful chemical reorganization and reinforcement.",
